diff --git a/Dawn/unit/AKIMBO-TESTS.md b/Dawn/unit/AKIMBO-TESTS.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..7b1f431a3dfe46f8358287bc94dea5732aa0f6ac
--- /dev/null
+++ b/Dawn/unit/AKIMBO-TESTS.md
@@ -0,0 +1,281 @@
+# Generated akimbo checks
+
+These headless tests use synthetic state and a public descriptor fixture. They do
+not need game assets or a running process. From this directory in an x64 Visual
+Studio developer prompt, compile with C++20, `/W4 /WX`, and run each executable:
+
+```bat
+cl /nologo /std:c++20 /EHsc /O2 /W4 /WX akimbo_descriptor_tests.cpp
+akimbo_descriptor_tests.exe
+
+cl /nologo /std:c++20 /EHsc /O2 /W4 /WX akimbo_firing_tests.cpp ..\src\client\hooks\akimbo\firing.cpp
+akimbo_firing_tests.exe
+
+cl /nologo /std:c++20 /EHsc /O2 /W4 /WX akimbo_magazine_tests.cpp
+akimbo_magazine_tests.exe
+
+cl /nologo /std:c++20 /EHsc /O2 /W4 /WX akimbo_infinite_ammo_tests.cpp
+akimbo_infinite_ammo_tests.exe
+
+cl /nologo /std:c++20 /EHsc /O2 /W4 /WX akimbo_placement_tests.cpp ..\src\client\hooks\akimbo\pose_math.cpp
+akimbo_placement_tests.exe
+
+cl /nologo /std:c++20 /EHsc /O2 /W4 /WX akimbo_hud_tests.cpp
+akimbo_hud_tests.exe
+
+cl /nologo /std:c++20 /EHsc /O2 /W4 /WX akimbo_native_hud_tests.cpp
+akimbo_native_hud_tests.exe
+
+cl /nologo /std:c++20 /EHsc /O2 /W4 /WX /I..\vendor\imgui akimbo_hud_render_tests.cpp ..\vendor\imgui\imgui.cpp ..\vendor\imgui\imgui_draw.cpp ..\vendor\imgui\imgui_tables.cpp ..\vendor\imgui\imgui_widgets.cpp
+akimbo_hud_render_tests.exe
+```
+
+The descriptor fixture is shared with Sundial's `native-bindings-v5.json` contract.
+It checks bounded explicit spacing, reload policy, version mismatch, malformed fields and native
+profile validation. Placement and firing tests live alongside it and exercise
+the production math and dispatch implementations.
+The placement suite checks 50, 190, 360 and 800 mm gaps with a rotated and translated
+camera, all 22 mirrored arm/finger bones, the reticle midpoint, marker/FX agreement,
+native/camera scales 0.97, 1.0 and 1.03, resident-pass idempotence and unchanged legacy placement. The firing suite checks
+independent hand selection, fair alternation, failed tickets and exact world muzzle
+placement while preserving the native orientation and opaque origin fields.
+
+The magazine suite exercises the production state and native-transfer adapter:
+policy 1 retains empty-only reloads; policy 2 reloads all non-full hands when R is
+pressed. Policy 3 reloads the hand with fewer rounds (left on equal nonzero
+counts), and allows both to reload only when both are empty. A fresh R can add the
+second empty hand without restarting the first timer. Otherwise R during an
+active reload does not add a hand; holding R through completion does not select another.
+Each hand keeps its remaining rounds and its own tactical/empty native
+duration. Reserve is charged only for added rounds. A completed hand can fire
+while the other reloads. Synthetic native stores check generation changes,
+changed baselines, failed setters and a conditional reserve refund. Tests also
+cover a native shot paid before its queued emission, retained hand selection,
+canceled/refunded queues, deferred R presses, reload progress during repeated
+other-hand shots, swaps, interruptions, native count changes and conservation
+through randomized interleaved firing/reload sequences. Reload-property tests
+validate relocated typed records using synthetic engine handle tables.
+Repeated component-storage moves retain one state for the same generation-bearing
+handle, owner and generated model, including reload clocks and queued-shot ownership.
+The captured partial/empty cases check that moves cannot refill early, restart a
+timer or charge another initial reserve fill. Changed generations remain separate;
+exact old-pointer snapshots and native transactions still fail after a move.
+
+The infinite-ammo integration test compiles Dawn's actual setter callbacks and
+effective reserve-policy accessor with synthetic native trampolines. It reproduces
+the finite-debit mismatch when the enabled feature restores reserve to 500, then
+checks initialization and independent partial reloads under that explicit policy.
+The hand magazines remain finite and require reloading. It also checks ordinary
+finite reserve conservation, activity restrictions and policy changes during a
+transaction. These checks do not change the player's infinite-ammo setting.
+
+The HUD snapshot tests register the in-game carrier and another allocated model, then check
+owner/content/variant/generation bindings, native icon binding, separate rounds,
+independent reload progress and completion, and malformed snapshots. Layout checks
+cover small, 16:9, ultrawide and 4K viewports. The historical render test uses Dawn's real ImGui
+code and embedded vector font without a graphics backend: it checks maximum count
+and reload text widths, emitted vertices, panel bounds and absence of input capture.
+The floating-panel renderer is retained only as an isolated regression fixture;
+production no longer invokes it or substitutes it when native UI guards decline.
+Presentation freshness is bound to the exact model, owner and controller. Cached
+icon geometry may remain stale for seconds without hiding a freshly presented
+weapon; stale presentation and switched owners still suppress the snapshot.
+
+The native HUD uses the game's existing loaded-ammo text and shadow widgets. Its
+two lines display `L26` and `R26`; only the digits of a reloading hand turn red.
+The shared native reserve display remains unchanged. Each layer keeps its native
+visibility and crossfade; the native layout constructor receives
+half its normal font size so both lines fit the existing count area. Both layers
+move upward by one completed-layout font height in native UI coordinates, so
+the game's transform and HUD scale apply. Labels and non-reloading digits retain
+their native colors. Reload digits use the source RGB brightness for red while
+preserving native alpha; a black shadow stays black. No widget or
+text-resource pointers are retained across callbacks, and native allocation and
+reference-counting continue to own the text and glyph layout.
+
+The native HUD suite checks exact child membership, reciprocal UI links, matching
+content-selected icons, direct UTF16 binding fields and bounded text restoration.
+It exercises the production caller/source routing helpers and a scheduler harness
+with the audited clear-before-callback ordering, including initial equip readiness,
+cached listeners, reload changes without a changed aggregate count, focus loss,
+stock-weapon restoration, placeholder rows and nested callbacks. Only the narrow
+loaded-counter widget family receives native update pulses. Native text invalidation
+occurs on a verified text difference; unchanged values retain the cached layout.
+The style checks exercise 0 through 200 with one-, two- and three-digit counts,
+both independent reload masks and both loading, native font sizes 9/18/36,
+repeated draws without cumulative movement, explicit alpha/black/colored styles,
+exact UTF16 glyph-to-hand mapping and refusal of malformed lists. A bounded
+stack clone supplies two line lists and at most eight glyphs to the native text
+submission function, which copies them into its command arena synchronously.
+Its style-allocation count covers every possible pointer transition, including
+clipped glyphs and recurring noncontiguous styles. Tests copy command values
+before the clone expires and verify that all source resources remain untouched.
+Text/layout caching and draw-command caching are modeled separately: native
+render-dirty publication updates red/normal colors even with unchanged counts.
+Native text updates can keep drawing an older layout while the next number is
+built. Positioning therefore recognizes the actual submitted two-line glyphs,
+independently of the pending input string, job pointer or update state. The submit
+callback verifies that the widget owns the actual layout at that moment; it does
+not retain a layout pointer sampled before the draw or across frames. Fresh
+owner/icon/capacity data separately gates reload tint. Missing tint data or an
+unsupported color style keeps the original native layout with the same upward
+offset. A retained pair also stays raised during focus/stock restoration until
+the real single-line stock layout replaces it.
+Transition tests cover native states 4/6/7/8, pending jobs, differing requested and
+visible counts, a layout-pointer swap inside the callback, optional tint failure,
+and stock restoration. Malformed or unowned layouts pass through unchanged.
+The exact native update, comparison, UTF16 factory, font-config, draw and submit ABIs are
+guarded against the supported executable. These source checks cannot establish
+the absence of visual transition defects; the retained-layout correction still
+requires an in-game check.
+
+Hand placement and independent firing were confirmed in game. The first ammo
+playtest exposed infinite-reserve setter and cached-icon integration defects.
+The reserve-policy correction restored initial fills and both firing hands in
+game. Subsequent read-only samples identified the same logical weapon component
+moving among native storage addresses; pointer-keyed state had restarted reloads
+and initial fills. Shooting and lower-magazine reload behavior were subsequently accepted in game.
+The raised native counter position and reload-digit tint were subsequently
+accepted in game. Number changes exposed a brief return to the original lower
+position while an older glyph layout was retained. The correction preserves the
+accepted steady appearance and has the transition coverage described above;
+its next in-game check remains pending.
+Descriptor publication alone does not prove that a chosen art assignment is loaded.
+
+Generic local FX attachment coverage is source-only and uses the production
+descriptor parser, handle resolver, sequence/selector readers and patch builder:
+
+```bat
+cl /nologo /std:c++20 /EHsc /O2 /W4 /WX akimbo_fx_tests.cpp /Fe:akimbo_fx_tests.exe
+akimbo_fx_tests.exe
+cl /nologo /std:c++20 /EHsc /O2 /W4 /WX akimbo_descriptor_tests.cpp /Fe:akimbo_descriptor_tests.exe /link /STACK:65536
+akimbo_descriptor_tests.exe
+```
+
+The FX suite covers every one-to-six live marker count within one-to-six native
+capacity slots, both hands, multiple role groups, translated/rotated source
+frames, endpoint and auxiliary storage, and unchanged source bytes. Mismatched
+population/capacity, endpoint offsets, selector bindings, particle tags and an
+owner change during the read reject without publishing a partial patch. Its
+identity callback is a controlled test seam; the retained identity regression
+separately exercises the full native owner walk. The descriptor suite exercises
+the optional additional-marker groups, frame validation and parser bounds,
+rejects them for adapter 7, and runs with a 64 KiB stack while keeping the large
+descriptor on the heap. Production descriptor and published registry storage
+are static. These checks do not establish live particle appearance.
+
+The independent burst clocks have separate portable tests:
+
+```bat
+cl /nologo /std:c++20 /EHsc /O2 /W4 /WX akimbo_burst_phase_tests.cpp /Fe:akimbo_burst_phase_tests.exe
+akimbo_burst_phase_tests.exe
+cl /nologo /std:c++20 /EHsc /O2 /W4 /WX akimbo_burst_cadence_tests.cpp /Fe:akimbo_burst_cadence_tests.exe
+akimbo_burst_cadence_tests.exe
+```
+
+The phase-bank suite checks exact storage/generation leases, stale commits,
+relocation, bounded double reads, retained native intervals and untouched shared
+epoch/queue/property bytes. The clock suite checks two unequal burst intervals
+across different frame subdivisions, one shared elapsed-time total, fair order
+at tied deadlines, an idle other hand, zero-time cycles and callback failures.
+These helpers are exercised together by the native session suite below. The
+separate adapter 11 qualifies digital uncharged trigger latches, source counts,
+ordinary reload storage and the exact owner before invoking the native phase.
+Auxiliary external recovery states remain rejected. Unit tests do not establish
+in-game timing, presentation or callback scheduling.
+
+The native burst-exit debit has its own production helper and regression:
+
+```bat
+cl /nologo /std:c++20 /EHsc /O2 /W4 /WX akimbo_burst_exit_tests.cpp /Fe:akimbo_burst_exit_tests.exe
+akimbo_burst_exit_tests.exe
+```
+
+Native `CB5DF0` reads loaded ammunition at return site `CB5F62` and consumes the
+remainder modulo the native burst count. Within a qualified phase scope that
+getter returns the selected hand's available count (zero while that hand is reloading). The helper observes the resulting
+native aggregate debit exactly once, after the already-settled shot costs. Tests
+cover every count 2–64 and magazine count 0–200, both hands, queued paid shots,
+selected-hand interruption without discarded rounds, other-hand reload preservation, setter refusal, wrong deltas, stale revisions,
+storage relocation and generation replacement. Missing qualification never
+publishes a hand scope. The phase bridge refuses a rejected getter callback even when no debit was captured.
+
+
+The finite-burst session uses the production request, clock and debit/receipt
+helpers with mutable native callback fixtures:
+
+```bat
+cl /nologo /std:c++20 /EHsc /O2 /W4 /WX akimbo_burst_session_tests.cpp /Fe:akimbo_burst_session_tests.exe
+akimbo_burst_session_tests.exe
+cl /nologo /std:c++20 /EHsc /O2 /W4 /WX akimbo_burst_driver_tests.cpp /Fe:akimbo_burst_driver_tests.exe
+akimbo_burst_driver_tests.exe
+cl /nologo /std:c++20 /EHsc /O2 /W4 /WX akimbo_burst_order_tests.cpp /Fe:akimbo_burst_order_tests.exe
+akimbo_burst_order_tests.exe
+```
+
+Each button owns a complete native burst. Holding both alternates those bursts;
+a fresh native inter-round interval separates the final round of one hand from
+the first round of the other. Neither the internal burst interval nor this
+handoff interval is halved. Both recovery clocks advance while one hand waits.
+Coverage includes long frames, tiny positive intervals, release/repress,
+last-round-empty boundaries, exact pointer relocation, rejection at every
+callback/write boundary, retained paid queues, once-only ammo debits and an
+unchanged opposite reload. A ninth owner cannot evict an active burst or paid
+receipt. Native start/stop effects and property clamps can run at zero delta;
+shared zero-time calls are deduplicated by aggregate activity, not repeated for
+each hand. Native CBF240 finalization runs once, and its aggregate request clear
+cannot erase a pending request belonging to the opposite hand.
+
+The guard tests mutate every pinned entry/caller byte and refuse missing reads.
+Actual native callbacks remain guarded by the supported build and source graph;
+the simulated callback tests do not execute the game engine. A runtime exposing
+only adapters 7/10 cannot install an adapter 11 package.
+
+Adapter 11 requires a privately emitted core as well as a private model. The
+production loader rejects a stock/source-only core, and the startup barrier
+recognizes that private tag before presentation. This barrier grants no execution
+permission: typed owner, definition, live counts and source checks still precede
+every borrowed callback. A partially initialized private root cannot fall through
+to the stock shared trigger. Direct adapters 7/10 keep their previous admission.
+
+The trigger copy and fresh idle-to-active boundary use checked per-hand magazine
+availability. An unavailable pending request remains durable but cannot win the
+next-burst tie. If reload begins during a burst, the original native failed-shot
+cleanup runs with zero available rounds for that hand; its actual loaded count,
+loading mask and revisions remain pinned through settlement. The other hand can
+fire and existing paid queue receipts survive. Semantic qualification failures
+are explicitly rejected, not mistaken for ordinary empty/reload readiness.
+The current generated capability set is 7/10/11 with schema 5. Descriptor tests
+cover the private-core append boundary (6467 refused, 6468 accepted), authored
+standalone bounds, and startup interception before presentation. Production
+loader tests additionally require that the core's package is an emitted artifact;
+coverage by stock dependency pins alone is insufficient. The source companion
+includes schema-2/3/4 fixtures as historical contract examples; current parser
+acceptance uses only schema 5.
+
+Tube reload scheduling and the source-only charge prototypes can be tested without
+assets:
+
+```bat
+cl /nologo /std:c++20 /EHsc /O2 /W4 /WX akimbo_tube_reload_tests.cpp /Fe:akimbo_tube_reload_tests.exe
+akimbo_tube_reload_tests.exe
+cl /nologo /std:c++20 /EHsc /O2 /W4 /WX akimbo_burst_tests.cpp /Fe:akimbo_burst_tests.exe
+akimbo_burst_tests.exe
+cl /nologo /std:c++20 /EHsc /O2 /W4 /WX akimbo_burst_trigger_tests.cpp /Fe:akimbo_burst_trigger_tests.exe
+akimbo_burst_trigger_tests.exe
+cl /nologo /std:c++20 /EHsc /O2 /W4 /WX akimbo_charge_tests.cpp /Fe:akimbo_charge_tests.exe
+akimbo_charge_tests.exe
+cl /nologo /std:c++20 /EHsc /O2 /W4 /WX akimbo_charge_layout_tests.cpp /Fe:akimbo_charge_layout_tests.exe
+akimbo_charge_layout_tests.exe
+```
+
+The tube suite checks native timing/property qualification, per-shell progression,
+reserve-limited transfers, queued emissions and relocation. The charge suites
+cover isolated phase/cancel and typed source-layout helpers only. They do not
+install native charge hooks or establish independent charged-weapon support.
+
+Release loader/lifecycle checks with 33 actual profiles and their actual package
+bytes passed alongside synthetic regressions. Those checks establish parsing,
+ownership, hash verification and startup/shutdown; they do not execute in-game
+weapon callbacks. Gameplay validation of the new cross-family/burst checkpoint
+remains pending.
